  "slug": "decoding-pathology-17b5",
  "summary": "New preprints use foundation models to recover molecular state from routine histology and to push spatial transcriptomics to single-cell resolution.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational recovery of single-cell molecular state from tissue measurements. Three preprints treat H&E as a molecular readout. Loki2, trained on 15 million cells, tests how much molecular and cellular state routine histology encodes. GenVinci reconstructs 3D molecular tissue scenes and maps senescence across aging mouse organs and human skin. sc-HistoMorphomics links 194 nuclear morphometric features to spatial transcriptomics. Spatial methods also move to cellular and subcellular resolution. st2sc infers single-cell expression from multicellular spots, a rotation-equivariant network learns 3D subcellular transcript embeddings, and CIRCA recovers identifiable cell and niche programs tied to plaque pathology. Representation learning continues to scale. Bloom unifies expression and chromatin accessibility across 300,000 mouse brain cells, MCT models cell sets to learn gene co-regulation, and scUNVEIL pretrains on about 92 million human cells via next-UMI prediction. Other work targets perturbation and dynamics. GFM predicts responses to unseen Perturb-seq perturbations, DRAFT infers dynamic GRNs, and CIRCE infers graded signaling strength.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-22"
}
